The '''1999 Football League Second Division play-off final''' was an [[association football]] match which was played on 30 May 1999 at [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|Wembley Stadium]], London, between [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] and [[Gillingham F.C.|Gillingham]].  The match was to determine the third and final team to gain [[Promotion and relegation|promotion]] from the [[Football League Second Division]], the third tier of [[English football league system|English football]], to the [[Football League First Division|First Division]].  The top two teams of the [[1998–99 Football League Second Division]] season gained automatic promotion to the First Division, while the clubs placed from third to sixth in the table took part in play-offs.  Manchester City ended the season in third position while Gillingham  were fourth.  The winners of these semi-finals competed for the final place in the First Division for the [[1999–2000 Football League First Division|1999–2000 season]].  In the semi-finals, Gillingham defeated [[Preston North End F.C.|Preston North End]] and Manchester City beat [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]].

It was Gillingham's first match at Wembley Stadium, although Manchester City had played there on eleven previous occasions.   The final drew a crowd of just under 77,000, the largest crowd to ever witness a Gillingham match, and was refereed by [[Mark Halsey]].  The match was scoreless until the 81st minute, when [[Carl Asaba]] gave Gillingham the lead. [[Robert Taylor (footballer, born 1971)|Robert Taylor]] added a second goal five minutes later.  [[Kevin Horlock]] scored for City in the 90th minute to halve the deficit and, in the fifth minute of [[injury time]], [[Paul Dickov]] scored an equaliser to make the score 2&ndash;2 and send the game into [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]].  With no further goals being scored, the match was decided by a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]], which City won 3&ndash;1 to gain promotion.

Manchester City's [[1999–2000 Manchester City F.C. season|next season]] saw them secure automatic promotion to the [[2000–01 FA Premier League|FA Premier League]] after ending their campaign as runners-up. Gillingham finished their [[1999–2000 Gillingham F.C. season|following season]] in third place in the Second Division and qualified for [[2000 Football League play-offs|the play-offs]] again, where they secured promotion to the First Division with a 3–2 victory against Wigan Athletic in [[2000 Football League Second Division play-off final|the final]].

