The teams finishing in the top two positions in the [[Football League Second Division|Second Division]], the third tier of the [[English football league system]], in the [[1998–99 Football League|1998–99 Football League season]] gained automatic [[Promotion and relegation|promotion]] to the [[Football League First Division|First Division]].<ref name=RS>{{cite web|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/eng99.html|title=England 1998/99|website=[[RSSSF]]|date=20 December 1999|access-date=10 April 2021|archive-date=23 October 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181023130001/http://www.rsssf.com/tablese/eng99.html|url-status=live}}</ref>  The teams finishing between third and sixth inclusive competed in the [[1999 Football League play-offs|play-offs]] for the third and final promotion place.<ref name=RS />  [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] finished the regular season in third place, two points ahead of fourth-placed [[Gillingham F.C.|Gillingham]].<ref name=RS />  Both therefore missed out on the two automatic places for promotion to the First Division and instead took part in the play-offs, along with [[Preston North End F.C.|Preston North End]] and [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]], to determine the third promoted team.  Manchester City finished five points behind [[Walsall F.C.|Walsall]] (who were promoted in second place) and nineteen behind league winners [[Fulham F.C.|Fulham]].<ref name=soccerway/>

Gillingham's opponents for their play-off semi-final were Preston North End, with the first match of the [[two-legged tie]] taking place at [[Deepdale]] in Preston on 16 May 1999. After a goalless first half, [[David Eyres]] put Preston ahead in the 54th minute.  [[Robert Taylor (footballer, born 1971)|Robert Taylor]] equalised with 11 minutes to go and the match ended 1–1.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://theguardian.newspapers.com/clip/74999770/the-guardian/ | work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 3 April 2021 | via = [[Newspapers.com]] | title = Gills equal to the task | date = 17 May 1999 | page = 28 | first = Ian | last = Winrow}}</ref>  The second leg was held three days later at [[Priestfield Stadium]] in Gillingham.  Within two minutes of the start of the match, Gillingham took the lead through [[Andy Hessenthaler]]: [[Barry Ashby]] passed to [[Carl Asaba]] who guided the ball to Hessenthaler to score.<ref name=guardian />  Gillingham dominated the remainder of the half and, despite pressure from Preston after the interval, they held onto their lead; [[Vince Bartram]], the Gillingham [[goalkeeper (association football)|goalkeeper]], dived full-length across his goal to keep out a strike from [[Jon Macken]].  The match ended 1–0 and Gillingham progressed to the final with a 2–1 [[aggregate score|aggregate victory]].<ref name=guardian>{{Cite web | url = https://theguardian.newspapers.com/clip/74999870/the-guardian/ |  work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 3 April 2021 | via = [[Newspapers.com]] | title = Hessenthaler leads men of Kent to Wembley | first = Trevor |last = Haylett | page = 32 | date = 20 May 1999}}</ref>

Manchester City faced Wigan Athletic in their semi-final and the first leg was played at [[Springfield Park (Wigan)|Springfield Park]] in Wigan on 15 May 1999.  Within 20 seconds, the home side had taken the lead: a misunderstanding between Manchester City goalkeeper [[Nicky Weaver]] and his team-mate [[Gerard Wiekens]] allowed [[Stuart Barlow]] to score from {{convert|15|yd}}.  [[Paul Dickov]] equalised with 13 minutes of the match remaining and the game ended 1–1.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://theguardian.newspapers.com/clip/259580455/the-guardian/ | work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 3 April 2021 | via = [[Newspapers.com]] | title = Dickov quells fear of the faithful | date = 17 May 1999 | page = 28 | first = Ian | last = Whittell }}{{Dead link|date=April 2024 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>  The second leg took place four days later at [[Maine Road]] in Manchester.  City dominated the early stages and took the lead in the 27th minute through [[Shaun Goater]], who put the ball past [[Roy Carroll]] in the Wigan goal with his chest from a [[Michael Brown (footballer, born 1977)|Michael Brown]] [[Cross (association football)|cross]].  With four minutes remaining, a shot by Wigan's [[Graeme Jones]] struck the crossbar.  The match ended 1–0 and Manchester City progressed to the final 2–1 on aggregate.<ref name=ashley>{{Cite web | url = https://theguardian.newspapers.com/clip/74999840/the-guardian/ |  work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 3 April 2021 | via = [[Newspapers.com]] | title = Goater's goal makes City slicker | date = 20 May 1999 | page = 32 | first = Richard |last= Ashley}}</ref>

