==Match==
===Background===
The match was Gillingham's first appearance at [[Wembley Stadium]], the country's [[List of national stadiums|national stadium]] and the venue reserved for major tournament finals since 1923.<ref name=ellig>{{cite book  | last =  Elligate  | first = David  | title = Gillingham FC On This Day  | publisher = Pitch Publishing  | year = 2009  | isbn = 9781905411450|location=[[Durrington, West Sussex|Durrington]] |page=97 }}</ref>  Manchester City, by comparison, had played there on eleven previous occasions in [[FA Cup]] and [[EFL Cup|League Cup]] finals.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Nationwide Football Annual 2013–2014|date=2014|publisher=SportsBooks Limited|isbn=9781907524387|editor1-first=Stuart|editor1-last=Barnes|location=[[Cheltenham]]| pages=150, 155}}</ref>  Gillingham were aiming to reach the second tier of English football for the first time in their history,<ref name="ind">{{cite news|page=31|first=Pete|last=Lansley|title=Football: Taylor believes Gills will pass character test|newspaper=[[The Independent]]|date=27 May 2000|url=https://www.proquest.com/docview/311630440|url-access=subscription|access-date=11 February 2021|archive-date=30 March 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210330110946/https://www.proquest.com/docview/311630440/D6C4786F02BE4D3CPQ/1|id={{ProQuest|311630440}} |url-status=live}}</ref> whereas their opponents had spent more than 100 seasons in the top two tiers and had played in the top-level [[FA Premier League]] as recently as the [[1995–96 in English football|1995–96]] season.<ref>{{cite book|title=The History of English Football Clubs|page=181|first1=Colin|last1=Mitchell|first2=Jon |last2=Reeves|first3=Daniel|last3=Tyler|publisher=[[New Holland Publishers]]|location=London|date=2013|isbn=9781780094496}}</ref>  At the end of the [[1997–98 in English football|1997–98]] season, City had been [[promotion and relegation|relegated]] to the third tier of English football for the first time.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/man-city-1998-relegation-side-18179348|title=What happened to the Man City team relegated from the First Division in 1998?|first=Carrie|last=Dunn|work=[[Manchester Evening News]]|date=2 May 2020|access-date=1 April 2021|archive-date=26 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210426143321/https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/man-city-1998-relegation-side-18179348|url-status=live}}</ref>

This season saw the first competitive matches between the sides: the first game, at Maine Road in November 1998, ended in a goalless [[Tie (draw)|draw]] while the return match played at Priestfield the following April ended in a 2–0 victory for Manchester City.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/teams/manchester-city/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Gillingham/ | website = 11v11 | publisher = AFS Enterprises | access-date = 3 April 2021 | title = Manchester City football club: record v Gillingham | archive-date = 26 September 2019 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20190926195949/https://www.11v11.com/teams/manchester-city/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Gillingham/ | url-status = live }}</ref>  Asaba was the season's top scorer for Gillingham with 22 goals (20 in the league and 2 in the [[1998–99 Football League Trophy|Football League Trophy]]) followed by Taylor, who scored 19 goals in the regular season (16 in the league and 3 in the Football League Trophy).<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=1098&teamTabs=stats&season_id=128 | website = Soccerbase | publisher = Centurycomm | access-date = 3 April 2021 | title = Gillingham squad details – 1998/99 – Player appearances}}</ref>  Manchester City's Goater was leading scorer for his side, with 20 goals (17 in the league, 1 in the [[1998–99 FA Cup|FA Cup]] and 2 in the [[1998–99 Football League Cup|League Cup]]) followed by Dickov on 13 (10 in the league, 1 in the FA Cup and 2 in the League Cup) and [[Kevin Horlock]] with 10 (all in the league).<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=1718&teamTabs=stats&season_id=128 | website = Soccerbase | publisher = Centurycomm | access-date = 3 April 2021 | title = Man City squad details – 1998/99 – Player appearances | archive-date = 27 July 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210727150723/https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=1718&teamTabs=stats&season_id=128 | url-status = live }}</ref>

Gillingham [[manager (association football)|manager]] [[Tony Pulis]] picked the same eleven players in his starting line-up as in the second leg of the semi-final.<ref name=guardian/>  His opposite number, [[Joe Royle]], made one change, selecting [[Andy Morrison]] in place of [[Tony Vaughan (footballer)|Tony Vaughan]], who was instead named as one of the [[substitute (association football)|substitute]]s.<ref name=ashley />  The [[Referee (association football)|referee]] for the match was [[Mark Halsey]], from [[Welwyn Garden City]].<ref name=elevrep>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/matches/gillingham-v-manchester-city-30-may-1999-307136/ | website = 11v11 | publisher = AFS Enterprises | access-date = 3 April 2021 | title = Gillingham v Manchester City, 30 May 1999 | archive-date = 27 July 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210727150711/https://www.11v11.com/matches/gillingham-v-manchester-city-30-may-1999-307136/ | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=guardrep/>  The play-off final drew an attendance of 76,935, the largest crowd ever to watch a Gillingham match.<ref name=ellig />  It was a new record attendance for the third-tier play-off final and a larger attendance than that for [[1999 Football League First Division play-off final|the First Division play-off final]] the following day.<ref>{{cite book|pages=45–46|first=Stuart|last=Barnes|date=2009|title=Nationwide Football Annual 2009–2010|publisher=[[SportsBooks Limited]]|location=[[Cheltenham]] |isbn=9781899807819}}</ref>  [[Liam Gallagher|Liam]] and [[Noel Gallagher]] of the rock band [[Oasis (band)|Oasis]] were among the Manchester City fans in attendance.<ref name=BBC>{{cite web|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/356672.stm|title=Sport: Football: Shoot-out success for City|publisher=[[BBC News]]|date=30 May 1999|access-date=4 April 2021|archive-date=2 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210402082849/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/356672.stm|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Summary===
[[File:Paul Dickov (cropped).png|thumb|right|upright|alt=Footballer Paul Dickov|[[Paul Dickov]] ''(pictured in 2008)'' scored the equalising goal five minutes into injury time.]]
The match [[Kick-off (association football)|kicked off]] at around 3.00{{nbsp}}p.m. on 30 May 1999.<ref name=elevrep/><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://theguardian.newspapers.com/clip/82279156/the-observer/ | via = [[Newspapers.com]]| work = [[The Observer]] | title = Royle's antidote for Cityitis bug| access-date = 27 July 2021 | page = 74 | first = Amy | last = Lawrence | date = 30 May 1999}}</ref> In the first minute, Dickov's [[bicycle kick|overhead kick]] struck Ashby's hand in the Gillingham [[penalty area]] but referee Halsey did not award a [[penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]].  Goater then saw his shot hit the Gillingham goalpost.  Gillingham's first goal-scoring chance came from [[Mick Galloway]], who had a shot from close range saved by a diving Weaver.<ref name=indept>{{Cite web | url = https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A66592371/STND?u=wikipedia&sid=bookmark-STND&xid=9833beb6 | work = [[The Independent]] | access-date = 19 July 2021 | via = [[Gale (publisher)|Gale]] | title = Football: Royle's Blues rival United; Gillingham 2 Manchester City 2 (aet; Manchester City win 3–1 on penalties) | first = Conrad | last = Leach | date = 31 May 1999 | url-access = subscription | archive-date = 27 July 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210727150713/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=STND&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CA66592371&v=2.1&it=r&sid=bookmark-STND&asid=9833beb6 | url-status = live }}</ref>  After 26 minutes, City's [[Terry Cooke]] crossed the ball into the penalty area, where Horlock headed it towards goal, but it was saved by Gillingham's Bartram.<ref name=indept />  Both teams had further goal-scoring opportunities in the second half.  For Gillingham, [[Nicky Southall]] crossed the ball to [[Mark Saunders (footballer)|Mark Saunders]], but his shot went wide of the goal, and Asaba was [[Tackle (football move)|tackled]] by City's [[Ian Bishop (footballer)|Ian Bishop]] when in a potential scoring position.<ref name=indept />  After 70 minutes, Weaver saved a strongly-hit shot from Gillingham's [[Paul Smith (footballer, born 1971)|Paul Smith]], and five minutes later Cooke was again involved for City, as he set up Goater, whose shot went past Bartram but hit the goalpost.<ref name=indept />  The [[BBC]] characterised much of the match as "ordinary" and suggested that both teams were somewhat overawed by the high-profile setting.<ref name=BBC />

The match remained scoreless until the 81st minute, when Asaba gave Gillingham the lead.  He played a [[push and run|one-two]] with Smith before shooting past Weaver from around {{convert|15|yd}}.  Taylor added a second goal five minutes later: he found Asaba with a [[Header (association football)|header]] and received a backheeled return pass before scoring with a shot.<ref name=timesrep/>  With only a minute of normal time left, and two goals behind in the game, many City fans considered that the game had been lost and began to make their way to the exits.<ref name=BBC />   However, after [[Darren Carr]] had tackled Goater to deny him a goal-scoring opportunity, Horlock scored from the rebound for City to halve the deficit in the 90th minute.  In the fifth minute of [[injury time]], Wiekens punted the ball forward, Horlock and Goater helped it on to Dickov who scored an equalising goal.  The regulation 90 minutes ended moments later, and with the scores level the game went into [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]].<ref name=timesrep/>  Dickov saw his header gathered by Bartram and although Hodge's cross was blocked by [[Jeff Whitley]]'s hand, no penalty was awarded.  With no goals being scored in the additional 30 minutes, the match and promotion to the First Division would be decided by a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]].<ref name=BBC />

Horlock scored the opening penalty for Manchester City before Weaver saved Smith's spot kick.  Dickov missed his penalty after it struck both posts, but [[Adrian Pennock]] then missed for Gillingham.  Cooke made it 2–0 to City before [[John Hodge (English footballer)|John Hodge]] scored for Gillingham. [[Richard Edghill]] scored for City to make the score 3–1, meaning that City would win if [[Guy Butters]] failed to score with Gillingham's fourth penalty.  Weaver saved the defender's kick to give Manchester City victory and secure promotion to the First Division.<ref name=guardrep>{{Cite web | url = https://theguardian.newspapers.com/clip/74999921/the-guardian/ | work = [[The Guardian]] | via = [[Newspapers.com]] | access-date = 3 April 2021 | title = Now City turn lost cause into a triumph | first = Trevor | last = Haylett | date = 31 May 1999 | page = 25 | archive-date = 27 July 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210727150752/https://theguardian.newspapers.com/clip/74999921/the-guardian/ | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=timesrep>{{Cite news | url = https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/IF0500231797/TTDA?u=wikipedia&sid=TTDA&xid=5f472bb6 | work = [[The Times]] | via = [[Gale (publisher)|Gale]] | title = City stage another Manchester late show | page = 31 | date = 31 May 1999 | first = Keith | last = Pike | url-access = subscription | access-date = 3 April 2021 | archive-date = 27 July 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210727150804/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?p=TTDA&u=wikipedia&id=GALE%7CIF0500231797&v=2.1&it=r&sid=TTDA&asid=5f472bb6 | url-status = live }}</ref>

