==Post-match==
[[File:Tony_pulis_2014.jpg|thumb|right|upright|alt=Football manager Tony Pulis|Gillingham manager [[Tony Pulis]] ''(pictured in 2014)'' was dismissed from his job in controversial circumstances one month after the final.]]
Gillingham manager Pulis was bemused by the amount of time added on by referee Halsey, stating that he "could not believe" the decision to add an additional five minutes.<ref name=timesrep />  One month after the 1999 final, Pulis was dismissed from his job at Gillingham, amid accusations of gross misconduct on his part, a decision which led to a lengthy and acrimonious court case between Pulis and club owner [[Paul Scally]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/apr/26/newsstory.sport1|title=Pulis 'drank champagne' after sacking|work=[[The Guardian]]|first=Jon|last=Brodkin|date=26 April 2001|access-date=1 April 2021}}</ref>  Scally was charged with misconduct by [[the Football Association]] (the FA), the governing body of the sport in England, after it emerged that he had placed a series of bets on the play-off final in contravention of the rules governing individuals gambling on matches in which they had a vested interest.  He appeared at an FA tribunal the following year and was fined £10,000.<ref name=ellig2>{{cite book  | last =  Elligate  | first = David  | title = Gillingham FC On This Day  | publisher = Pitch Publishing  | year = 2009  | isbn = 9781905411450|location=[[Durrington, West Sussex|Durrington]] |page=116 }}</ref>

Manchester City's [[1999–2000 Manchester City F.C. season|next season]] saw them secure automatic promotion to the FA Premier League after ending their campaign as runners-up.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://uk.soccerway.com/national/england/championship/1999-2000/regular-season/r533/ | website = Soccerway | publisher = Perform Group | access-date = 3 April 2021 | title = Championship – 1999/2000 – Regular season | archive-date = 27 July 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210727150728/https://uk.soccerway.com/national/england/championship/1999-2000/regular-season/r533/ | url-status = live }}</ref>   Gillingham, under new manager [[Peter Taylor (footballer, born 1953)|Peter Taylor]], returned to [[2000 Football League play-offs|the play-offs]] in their [[1999–2000 Gillingham F.C. season|following season]], finishing in third place in the Second Division.  They secured promotion to the First Division with a 3–2 victory against Wigan Athletic in [[2000 Football League Second Division play-off final|the final]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/league-tables/league-division-2/2000/ | website = 11v11 | publisher = AFS Enterprises | access-date = 3 April 2021 | title = League Division 2 end of season table for 1999–00 season | archive-date = 27 September 2018 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180927050225/https://www.11v11.com/league-tables/league-division-2/2000/ | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/football/767702.stm|title=Second time lucky for Gills|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|date=28 May 2000|access-date=4 July 2021|archive-date=3 March 2003|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20030303203928/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/football/767702.stm|url-status=live}}</ref>

Looking back on the match in 2011, Pulis stated that "[Gillingham] didn't deserve to lose that game{{nbsp}}... But it made me a much stronger person. You take things out of defeat as well as victory".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/stoke-boss-tony-pulis-out-858693|title=Stoke boss Tony Pulis out for Wembley revenge on Manchester City|work=[[Manchester Evening News]]|date=18 April 2011|access-date=10 April 2021|
archive-date=10 April 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210410185815/https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/stoke-boss-tony-pulis-out-858693|url-status=live}}</ref>  Manchester City's 1999 [[captain (association football)|captain]], Morrison, contended in 2018 that the club was at risk of going out of business within a year, if not for their match-winning promotion out of the Second Division.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/09/04/mark-halsey-denied-gillingham-manchester-citys-riches-dark-day/|title=How Mark Halsey denied Gillingham Manchester City's riches on a dark day in 1999|work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|first=Daniel|last=Schofield|date=4 September 2018|access-date=4 April 2021|archive-date=8 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180908202313/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/09/04/mark-halsey-denied-gillingham-manchester-citys-riches-dark-day/|url-status=live}}</ref>  Twenty years after the match, Dickov recalled scoring the equalising goal past his friend and the [[Groomsman|best man]] at his wedding, Gillingham goalkeeper Bartram: "There is nothing better than getting one over your mate or reminding him of it a few years later".<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/29/paul-dickov-manchester-city-1999-play-off-final-gillingham | work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 5 April 2021 | title = Twenty years after: Paul Dickov and Manchester City's 1999 play-off win | first = Jamie | last = Jackson | date = 29 May 2019 | archive-date = 31 March 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210331190130/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/29/paul-dickov-manchester-city-1999-play-off-final-gillingham | url-status = live }}</ref>  As a result of the subsequent success of the club, the ''[[Manchester Evening News]]'' described the match as "legendary in City's history" and Dickov's celebration of his goal as "iconic".<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/man-city-gillingham-wembley-1999-16347459 | work = [[Manchester Evening News]] | title = Man City's dramatic 1999 play-off victory keeps getting bigger | date = 30 May 2019 | access-date = 5 April 2021 | first = Simon | last = Bajkowski | archive-date = 29 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210529081801/https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/man-city-gillingham-wembley-1999-16347459 | url-status = live }}</ref>  In 2017, the [[English Football League]] (formerly The Football League) described the 1999 final as a "play-off classic".<ref>{{Cite news | url = https://www.efl.com/news/2017/may/play-off-classics-gillingham-v-manchester-city/ | publisher = [[English Football League]] | title = Play-Off Classics: Gillingham v Manchester City | date = 18 May 2017 | access-date = 5 April 2021 | archive-date = 24 January 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210124060937/https://www.efl.com/news/2017/may/play-off-classics-gillingham-v-manchester-city/ | url-status = live }}</ref>  Manchester City fans and other commentators regard the game as a crucial first step in the club's revival from the third tier of English football to yearly contenders for the Premier League championship.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2013/may/24/joy-of-six-play-off-finals |first=Barry |last=Glendenning |author-link=Barry Glendenning |title=The Joy of Six: Football League play-off finals |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=30 October 2013 |date=24 May 2013 |archive-date=2 November 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131102204817/http://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2013/may/24/joy-of-six-play-off-finals |url-status=live }}</ref>
